Super Star Trek 95 v1.1 (2026 bug-fix release)
==============================================

The original 1995 v1.0 code is kept unchanged in git at the tag
"strek95-orig". Version 1.1 fixes bugs only; the game rules and
balance are the same. Build with "make" (or "make CC=gcc").

Changes in v1.1:

 - You can no longer fly off the edge of the galaxy. Doing so used to
   add a "ghost" Klingon that could never be destroyed, so the game
   could not be won.
 - Fixed a phantom starbase: sector 1,1 of any quadrant without a base
   used to dock, refuel and protect the ship.
 - A destroyed starbase no longer refuels you or shows up on scans.
 - The computer's torpedo course now hits the target. About a third of
   its suggested shots used to miss (e.g. 134 instead of 135).
 - Torpedoes no longer slide along the bottom row of a quadrant.
 - Fixed a crash and garbage text in the Galactic Region Map and the
   "Now entering ..." quadrant names.
 - All input is read safely: no more gets() buffer overflows, and the
   game exits cleanly at end of input instead of looping forever.
 - Out-of-range or junk input (e.g. "nan", "abc", course 500) is
   refused instead of crashing, dropping shields, or flying north.
 - A Klingon that moves keeps its damage instead of healing to full.
 - Leaving a starbase and stopping at a star no longer leaves the ship
   "Docked" (and shielded by the base).
 - The ship always sits on a whole sector, so the sensor display,
   torpedoes and docking agree on where it is.
 - Impulse travel now takes time (0.1 stardate per factor), as the
   integer division always made it 0.
 - A Klingon hit down to exactly 0 energy is now destroyed.
 - The score penalty for a damaging hit now uses the damage taken.
 - The "ins" command shows instruct.doc (it looked for a missing file).
 - Starbases are drawn as >!< as described in instruct.doc.
 - Smaller fixes: files are closed after reading, the rating restarts
   with each new game, and several off-by-one checks were corrected.
 - Builds cleanly with modern gcc and clang (C89 through C23); the
   Makefile now uses "cc" instead of "acc".

Original README follows.
----------------------------------------------------------------------


This is an original remake of an old BASIC program. It comes from the
classic book _BASIC Computer Games_ edited by David Ahl of Creative
Computing fame. It was published by Workman Publishing, 1 West 39 Street,
New York, New York, and the ISBN is: 0-89489-052-3.

Here is the original header:

10 REM SUPER STARTREK - MAY 16, 1978 - REQUIRES 24K MEMORY
30 REM
40 REM ****        **** STAR TREK ****        ****
50 REM **** SIMULATION OF A MISSION OF THE STARSHIP ENTERPRISE,
60 REM **** AS SEEN ON THE STAR TREK TV SHOW.
70 REM **** ORIGINAL PROGRAM BY MIKE MAYFIELD, MODIFIED VERSION
80 REM **** PUBLISHED IN DEC'S "101 BASIC GAMES", BY DAVE AHL.
90 REM **** MODIFICATIONS TO THE LATTER (PLUS DEBUGGING) BY BOB
100 REM *** LEEDOM - APRIL & DECEMBER 1974,
110 REM *** WITH A LITTLE HELP FROM HIS FRIENDS . . .
120 REM *** COMMENTS, EPITHETS, AND SUGGESTIONS SOLICITED --
130 REM *** SEND TO:  R. C. LEEDOM
140 REM ***           WESTINGHOUSE DEFENSE & ELECTRONICS SYSTEMS CNTR.
150 REM ***           BOX 746, M.S. 338
160 REM ***           BALTIMORE, MD  21203
170 REM ***
180 REM *** CONVERTED TO MICROSOFT 8 K BASIC 3/16/78 BY JOHN BORDERS
190 REM *** LINE NUMBERS FROM VERSION STREK7 OF 1/12/75 PRESERVED AS
200 REM *** MUCH AS POSSIBLE WHILE USING MULTIPLE STATMENTS PER LINE
205 REM *** SOME LINES ARE LONGER THAN 72 CHARACTERS; THIS WAS DONE
210 REM *** BY USING "?" INSTEAD OF PRINT WHEN ENTERING LINES
215 REM

This version is a total re-write in C. I wanted to do it because this
is the game that got me interested in computers many years ago. This
version is much different that the Star Trek game that comes with
BSD unix written by Eric Allman, of sendmail fame.

I have added some features such as impulse engines, ranks, sound (such
that it is), etc.

I also have another version of this program in C that is a straight
port of the original BASIC code.  It is not included here.

This game is actually a subset of another game I am working on which you
might notice if you look through the code.
There is some support for changing the dimensions. You can change
the size of the galaxy in "galaxy.h" and the size of a quadrant in
"space.h". Currently changing the size of the galaxy breaks the
galactic region map on the computer.

This code is written in ANSI C with nothing fancy, so it should
compile anywhere there is an ANSI C compiler. I have compiled it on
Sun OS, Solaris, AIX, Digital Unix, and HPUX.

Please send me any bug reports, bug fixes, enhancments, etc. Contact
me at:
 
  Chris Nystrom
 
Thanks!


